course: everyday
lesson: 118
title: Saving energy, pets at home and the bathroom
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Small habits at home add up: switching things off at the wall, taking short showers and deciding what you want before opening the fridge all save energy, water and money.
explain: Pets share our homes, but their bodies are different from ours: some foods and plants that are harmless to people are poisonous to dogs and cats.
explain: For example, chocolate, grapes, raisins and onions can make a dog very ill, and even a little pollen or water from a vase of lilies can kill a cat.
explain: The bathroom has its own good habits: your own toothbrush, the toilet lid down, hot hair tools switched off and the fan on after a steamy bath.
fact: TVs and other machines on standby still use electricity, so they are switched off at the wall when not in use.
fact: Short showers save water and the energy that heats it.
fact: Holding the fridge door open lets cold air out and makes the fridge warm up.
fact: Chocolate, grapes, raisins and onions are poisonous to dogs.
fact: Lilies are deadly to cats, even in small amounts.
fact: A hamster cage door must be closed every time, or the hamster can escape and get lost or hurt.
fact: Toddlers sometimes eat pet food from bowls on the floor and can choke or get ill.
fact: Putting the toilet seat down and closing the lid is polite, and stops toddlers falling in.
fact: Each person uses only their own toothbrush, because sharing spreads germs.
fact: Hair straighteners get very hot, so they are switched off and put on a heat mat.
fact: Steam in a bathroom makes damp, and damp grows mould, black spots on walls and ceilings, so the fan is turned on and the door opened after.
exercise: The TV is on standby all night. What is best? | Switch it off at the wall | Leave it on standby, it uses nothing | Turn up the volume | Cover it with a blanket
exercise: Why are short showers better than long ones? | They save water and the energy to heat it | They make your skin cleaner | Long showers wash the natural oils out of your hair for good | Short showers are always hotter
exercise: You stand with the fridge open while you choose a snack. Why is this wasteful? | Cold air escapes and the fridge warms up | The food slides forward and falls out of the fridge onto the floor | The light inside will break | The fridge door will fall off
exercise: Someone leaves a chocolate bar on the table near the dog. Why is that a problem? | Chocolate is poisonous to dogs | Dogs do not like the taste of chocolate | Chocolate makes dogs bark more | The dog will share it with the cat
exercise: Which of these is safe to keep in a house with a cat? | A vase with no lilies in it | A vase of lilies on the table | Lilies on the windowsill | A bunch of lilies by the cat's bed
exercise: You fed your hamster and are leaving the room. What must you check? | That the cage door is closed | That the light is off in the cage | That the hamster is asleep | That the food bowl is empty
exercise: Why should a toddler be watched near the dog's food bowl? | She may eat the pet food and choke or get ill | The dog food costs too much money to share with a toddler | She will scare the dog away | The bowl is too heavy to lift
exercise: Why put the toilet lid down after using it? | It is polite and toddlers cannot fall in | It makes the toilet flush much better the next time | It keeps the bathroom warm | Toilets break if the lid is up
exercise: Your brother cannot find his toothbrush and wants to use yours. What is right? | No, each person uses only their own toothbrush | Yes, if he rinses it first | Yes, brothers and sisters can share everything in the bathroom | Yes, if it is only once
exercise: Your sister leaves her hair straighteners on by the sink. What do you do? | Switch them off and put them on a heat mat | Leave them on so they stay ready | Put them on the towel next to the sink so they cool down | Pick them up by the hot plates
exercise: The bathroom has no window and is full of steam after a bath. What should you do? | Turn on the fan and open the door after | Close the door tightly to keep it warm | Leave the steam to dry by itself | Put a towel under the door
exercise: Why is it dangerous to leave a bowl of grapes where the dog can reach it? | Grapes are poisonous to dogs | Grapes make dogs too full for dinner | Dogs choke on grape stalks only | Grapes are too sour for dogs
test: A family in Scotland wants a lower electricity bill. Which habit helps? | Switching machines off at the wall | Leaving lights on in empty rooms so the bulbs last longer | Opening the fridge door often | Leaving the TV on standby
test: Which treat is safe to give a dog? | A dog biscuit made for dogs | A handful of raisins | A piece of chocolate cake | Onion rings
test: A kitten in Adelaide plays with a fallen lily flower. Why is that an emergency? | Lilies are deadly to cats | Lily flowers make cats sneeze only | Lilies make cats sleepy for an hour | The kitten will turn yellow from the pollen
test: Black spots appear on the bathroom ceiling. What most likely caused them? | Damp from steam that was not let out | Too much bright sunlight coming into the bathroom every day | Too much toilet paper | Water that was too cold
test: Why is sharing a toothbrush a bad idea? | Germs pass from one mouth to the other | The bristles wear out twice as fast when two people use them | Toothpaste stops working | Toothbrushes change colour
test: A hamster is missing from its cage. What probably happened? | The cage door was left open | Hamsters can walk through bars | It turned invisible at night | The cat opened the lock
test: Why should hair straighteners never be left on near a towel? | They can burn the towel and start a fire | Towels make straighteners go cold and stop working properly | The towel will straighten | They only work near water
test: Why is it a good idea to decide what you want before opening the fridge? | The door stays open for less time, so the fridge stays cold | The food tastes better and fresher when you choose it that way | The fridge light lasts longer | You can eat more food
test: dog's beggin for my grapes, just one? | No, grapes are poisonous to dogs | Yes, one grape is a healthy treat | Yes, if they're green | Only raisins are bad, not grapes
test: got mum lilies, the cat keeps sniffin them | Keep them away, lilies are deadly to cats | Fine, cats don't eat flowers | Only the roots are a problem | Only dangerous to dogs
test: forgot my toothbrush, can i use my brothers | No, each person uses their own | Yes, rinse it and it's fine | Yes, family germs don't count | Yes, if you use more toothpaste
test: tv on standby all nite, thats off right | No, it still uses power, switch it off at the wall | Yes, standby uses nothing | Yes, the little red light is only there to show it is plugged in | It only uses power in the day
explain: A girl in Texas tried to hug a neighbour's dog while it was eating; it bit her face, and she needed stitches.
explain: A teenager in Texas used a plugged-in hair dryer next to a full bath; it fell into the water, she got an electric shock and was taken to hospital.
fact: Most dog bites to children come from a dog they know, often at home.
fact: Leave a dog alone while it eats, because even a friendly dog may bite to guard its food.
fact: A deep cat scratch should be washed and shown to an adult, because it can get infected.
fact: Electric heaters, hair dryers and plugged-in things must never be near a bath or sink of water.
test: doggo eating, gonna give him a big hug | Leave him alone while he eats, he might bite | Hug him, he loves you | Take his bowl to play | Pat his head and stroke his ears while he eats his dinner
test: hair dryer in the bath while i'm in it, to warm up | Never, electricity and water can kill | Fine if it's on low | Fine if you hold it high | Only for a minute
test: cat scratched me deep and it's swollen | Wash it and show an adult, cat scratches can get infected | Ignore it | Scratch the cat back gently so it learns never to do that again | Put a sweet on it
test: did u hear Bella's cat licked lily pollen off its fur n died, she's in bits. real lesson? | Lilies are deadly to cats, so keep them out of the house | Only big lily bunches are dangerous to cats | Cats only get ill if they eat the whole flower, stem and leaves | Lilies are safe if the vase water is clean
test: my mate swears her dog ate a whole chocolate cake n "exploded". what's probs exaggerated | The exploding; chocolate really can make a dog very ill | That chocolate is poisonous to dogs | That the dog may need a vet | That dogs should never be given chocolate cake or chocolate treats
test: kids say a teen dropped her hair dryer in the bath n it was "cos she's dumb". pass it on? | No, it is cruel; the lesson is no plugged-in things near water | Yes, it is a funny story to share | Yes, but only to the people at school who already know who she is | Yes, as a warning that she is clumsy
exam: Identify the principal reason for which devices on standby ought to be switched off at the wall when not in use. | On standby they still use electricity | On standby they may record conversations | On standby they slowly charge the house | On standby they make the picture brighter
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning foods injurious to dogs. | Chocolate, grapes, raisins and onions are poisonous | Carrots, rice, plain chicken and water are poisonous | Only foods which contain sugar harm a dog | Foods safe for humans are always safe for dogs
exam: Whereas a bath has been taken and the bathroom is full of steam, determine the appropriate measure and its reason. | Use the fan and open the door, as damp grows mould | Close every window, as steam keeps the room warm | Leave it as it is, as steam cleans the tiles | Turn on the heater, as steam is good for paint
exam: Ascertain the reason for which each person ought to use solely their own toothbrush. | Sharing a toothbrush spreads germs | Shared toothbrushes wear out faster | Toothbrushes only fit one mouth shape | Sharing makes the toothpaste weaker
